      passage: "Ancient writings on wisdom often emphasize [1], the quality of staying calm and waiting rather than reacting immediately to frustration.\n\nMany traditions also highlight [2], a form of inner strength that allows a person to hold back strong emotions such as anger or desire.\n\nTexts that offer practical [3] are often valued precisely because they translate abstract ideals into concrete advice for daily life.\n\nUnderstanding one's own limitations is central to [4], the quality of staying modest despite success or achievement.\n\nAnother recurring theme is [5], acting consistently according to one's values even when no one else is watching.\n\nMaking thoughtful decisions over time slowly builds genuine [6] into how one's choices affect both oneself and others.\n\nSome collections of sayings function almost like a moral [7], a general rule meant to guide behavior across many different situations.\n\nReaders who study these texts carefully often develop a broader [8] on what truly matters in life.\n\nFollowing such advice consistently is considered by many traditions to be a form of personal [9], built through steady effort over time.\n\nUltimately, these writings present wisdom itself as a practical [10] rather than a purely academic subject.",
