      passage: "Investigators who study strange, unexplained events often describe themselves as professional [1], refusing to accept supernatural explanations without solid evidence.\n\nMany so-called [2] encounters, once carefully examined, turn out to have ordinary physical causes such as sound waves, gas leaks or poor lighting.\n\nResearchers have repeatedly worked to [3] famous ghost stories by showing that the strange events can be explained through science.\n\nIn some rare cases, a supposedly haunted location later turns out to be an elaborate [4] created deliberately to attract visitors or media attention.\n\nStress, exhaustion and fear can sometimes distort a person's [5] of sound and light, making ordinary shadows seem threatening.\n\nIn extreme cases, prolonged fear or isolation may even trigger a temporary [6], causing a person to see or hear things that are not actually present.\n\nOlder [7] about spirits and curses can make people more likely to interpret strange noises as something sinister.\n\nOccasionally, two unrelated strange events happening on the same night are simply an unlucky [8], not evidence of anything supernatural.\n\nPeople experiencing a sudden vivid [9] may genuinely believe they witnessed something impossible, even when no external event actually occurred.\n\nUltimately, most investigators agree that the real [10] worth studying is not ghosts themselves, but why so many humans are drawn to believe in them.",
